1. Compatibility for your exact setup

Your setup is Debian 12 (Bookworm) inside an LXC container on Proxmox. This is a reasonable lab environment for osTicket.

    Debian 12 provides PHP 8.2 and MariaDB 10.11. 
    Debian Wiki
    +1

    The official osTicket requirements list PHP 8.2–8.4 and MySQL 5.5 or newer. 
    osTicket 1.17.7 documentation
    +1

    You do not need PHP 8.4 just to get started; use Debian's packaged PHP 8.2.

One important correction to the earlier guide: for this installation, use the official osTicket release archive and its upload directory, rather than assuming the Git development checkout's deployment command is necessary.
2. Prepare your LXC container

Run the following commands inside the Debian container, not on the Proxmox host.
bash

cat /etc/os-release
id
ip -br address
df -h /

Check that the output identifies Debian 12, that you have root access or sudo, and that the container has enough disk space.

Update the container:
bash

apt update
apt full-upgrade -y
apt install -y sudo curl wget unzip ca-certificates

If you are logged in as a non-root user, prefix administrative commands with sudo.

If apt update fails, check the container's IP address, default route, and DNS before proceeding.
3. Install the Debian 12 software stack

Run:
bash

apt install -y \
  apache2 mariadb-server \
  libapache2-mod-php php-cli php-common \
  php-mysql php-gd php-imap php-intl \
  php-mbstring php-xml php-zip php-curl \
  php-apcu php-ldap

This uses Debian's default PHP packages, avoiding version-specific package names.

Enable the services:
bash

systemctl enable --now apache2
systemctl enable --now mariadb

Verify the PHP version and required extensions:
bash

php -v
php -m

Check that the module list includes mysqli, gd, imap, intl, mbstring, xml, zip, and curl.

If any module is missing, install its matching Debian package and restart Apache:
bash

systemctl restart apache2

4. Create the database

Open MariaDB:
bash

mariadb

Create a dedicated database and user:
sql

CREATE DATABASE osticket
  CHARACTER SET utf8mb4
  COLLATE utf8mb4_unicode_ci;

CREATE USER 'osticketuser'@'localhost'
  IDENTIFIED BY 'REPLACE_WITH_A_LONG_UNIQUE_PASSWORD';

GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON osticket.*
  TO 'osticketuser'@'localhost';

EXIT;

Replace the example password before running the SQL. Save it securely for the installer, but never place it in your GitHub repository.
5. Download the official osTicket release

On a computer with a browser, open the official osTicket download page  and download the release archive. Transfer that archive into your container using SCP or another trusted file-transfer method.

For example, if the archive is named osticket.zip and your container's IP is 192.168.1.50, run this from your workstation, adjusting the filename and IP:
bash

scp osticket.zip root@192.168.1.50:/tmp/

Inside the container:
bash

mkdir -p /var/www/osticket
unzip /tmp/osticket.zip -d /tmp/osticket-extract
find /tmp/osticket-extract -maxdepth 3 -type d -name upload

The last command should identify the release's upload directory. Copy its contents into the web root, replacing the source path below with the exact path returned by find:
bash

cp -a /tmp/osticket-extract/PATH/TO/upload/. /var/www/osticket/

Do not type PATH/TO/upload literally; use the actual path on your container.

The release archive's upload directory contains the application files intended for deployment. The official installation guide  describes this installation method. 
osTicket 1.17.7 documentation
+1
6. Configure Apache

Create the site configuration:
bash

nano /etc/apache2/sites-available/osticket.conf

Add:
apache

<VirtualHost *:80>
    ServerName osticket.local
    DocumentRoot /var/www/osticket

    <Directory /var/www/osticket>
        Options FollowSymLinks
        AllowOverride All
        Require all granted
    </Directory>

    ErrorLog ${APACHE_LOG_DIR}/osticket-error.log
    CustomLog ${APACHE_LOG_DIR}/osticket-access.log combined
</VirtualHost>

Enable the site:
bash

a2enmod rewrite
a2ensite osticket.conf
a2dissite 000-default.conf
apache2ctl configtest
systemctl reload apache2

You should see:
text

Syntax OK

Set baseline ownership and permissions:
bash

chown -R root:www-data /var/www/osticket
find /var/www/osticket -type d -exec chmod 755 {} \;
find /var/www/osticket -type f -exec chmod 644 {} \;

Find the configuration template:
bash

ls -l /var/www/osticket/include/ost-sampleconfig.php

If present, create the configuration file:
bash

cp /var/www/osticket/include/ost-sampleconfig.php \
   /var/www/osticket/include/ost-config.php

The installer needs temporary write access to this file. If it reports a permissions error, grant narrowly scoped temporary write access to the configuration file, then restore restrictive permissions after setup. Do not make the entire application directory writable by Apache.
7. Finish installation in your browser

Find the container's IP address:
bash

hostname -I

On another device on the same network, open:

http://YOUR-LXC-IP/

The osTicket installer should appear. Enter:

    Help desk name: IT Support Lab

    Database: osticket

    Database user: osticketuser

    Database host: localhost

    Database password: the one you created earlier

Use a unique administrator username and password. After the installer completes, follow its instructions to remove or disable the setup directory and secure include/ost-config.php.
8. Troubleshooting common Proxmox LXC issues

Symptom
	

Commands to investigate

Apache won't start
	

systemctl status apache2 --no-pager

Database won't start
	

systemctl status mariadb --no-pager

PHP extensions missing
	

php -m

Web page returns 403
	

tail -n 50 /var/log/apache2/osticket-error.log

Web page returns 500
	

tail -n 50 /var/log/apache2/osticket-error.log

Can't reach container
	

ip -br address; ip route

Database login fails
	

mariadb -u osticketuser -p osticket

For an LXC container specifically, also check:

    The container has a working network bridge and DNS.

    The container is not out of memory or disk space.

    Apache and MariaDB are running inside the container.

    You are not running installation commands on the Proxmox host by mistake.

You do not normally need to enable privileged-container mode just to run Apache, PHP, and MariaDB.
